lib/law/accountable-attention-and-publication.json:
{"kind":"koios.publication-law","id":"accountable-attention-and-publication","purpose":"Compete for useful attention without allowing demand, compression, generation, advertising, or delivery to manufacture warrant.","invariants":["Demand may create an assignment but never evidence.","A source statement remains attributable to its source until an authorized editorial act adopts a narrower newsroom claim.","No public surface may create a factual commitment not licensed by the article, its cited evidence, its uncertainty, or a declared editorial stance.","A manuscript is not publishable unless deterministic gates clear and the latest exact-manuscript independent editorial judgment accepts it.","Corrections, clarifications, updates, and retractions must propagate to every dependent headline, article, feed, card, sitemap, metadata record, and syndication surface that Koios controls.","Audience demand, attention, advertising, revenue, provider cost, and editorial warrant are recorded as distinct facts and never substituted for one another.","Newsroom learning may propose a replay, route review, observation period, or standards review from durable outcomes; it may not itself publish, execute the replay, alter a route, or amend law.","LLMs may produce prose, images, or bounded choices; deterministic code owns serialization, digests, admission, ledgers, and publication transitions.","A provider is selected by the cheapest route that satisfies the declared quality and evidence requirements; zero marginal price never waives those requirements.","No deployment target becomes authoritative without parity evidence, an accepted resource grant, a rollback route, and an explicit cutover act.","A public privacy surface must describe the requests the exact publication artifact actually emits; suspended advertising or attention may not be described as active, and active third-party carriers may not be described as absent.","Advertising units, placements, provider requests, seller declarations, performance receipts, and revenue enter Koios only under provider-authored or provider-accepted evidence; Koios never invents ads.txt authority."],"derivedFrom":["dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/claims-to-prose.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/news-topology.md"],"enforcedBy":["src/researching/writing/editing/standards.mts","src/researching/writing/editing/liss-editorial.mts","src/management/newsroom-learning.mts","src/researching/writing/editing/publishing/managing/circulating/index.mts","src/administrating/deploying/static-shadow.mts","src/administrating/deploying/static-profile-parity.mts"]}

lib/law/reported-analysis-genre-contract.json:
{"kind":"koios.genre-contract","id":"reported-analysis/v1","promise":"A timely report organized around one witnessed event, with Koios's systems interpretation visibly subordinate to and licensed by the reporting.","evidenceBudget":{"targetWords":{"minimum":800,"maximum":1100},"minimumDistinctAssertions":15,"minimumPrimaryAssertions":15,"maximumWordsPerAssertion":55,"law":"The requested length is licensed only when the admitted packet carries enough distinct source-bound assertions to sustain it. When the budget does not clear, acquire reporting or commission a narrower genre before writer inference."},"writerPromptLaws":["Write reported analysis, not a thematic essay: the event and the people, institutions, places, dates, quantities, documents, and decisions in the sources remain the article's spine.","Before drafting, silently inventory the packet's supported claims and distinct new details; every paragraph and every section must advance at least one of them rather than restating the frame.","Treat each source as evidence only for its own actors, place, time, scope, attribution, and modality; never use thematic similarity as a bridge between different events.","Koios's systems interpretation may explain a pattern made visible by supported facts, but it may not supply an unreported causal mechanism, motive, consequence, category, or analogy.","Use straight attribution when the source is an allegation, estimate, forecast, interpretation, institutional statement, or contested claim; do not launder it into Koios's voice.","A headline may intensify relevance but not certainty; it names the subject before withholding an answer and preserves material attribution, uncertainty, scope, and correction state.","The lede tells the reader what happened, where, to whom, and why it matters now; do not open with a universal thesis, scene-setting abstraction, or generic collapse language.","Do not create a section unless the packet supplies a distinct factual development for it; if fewer than three sections are supportable, refuse with REPORTING_NEEDED instead of padding.","The ending must close on the most consequential supported unresolved fact, decision, deadline, conflict, or question facing the named actors; never substitute an abstract meditation for reporting.","Give readers enough factual payoff in the headline, description, summary, and lede to make an informed click or no-click decision; withhold an answer when useful, never the subject."],"gates":["event spine","paragraph-level source warrant","attribution and modality preservation","headline-body congruity","non-repetition","correction readiness","exact-manuscript independent editorial acceptance"],"derivedFrom":["dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/journalism.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/circulation-surface.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/clickbait.md"]}

INDEPENDENT EDITOR REPORT:
DISPOSITION: REVISE
PUBLISHABLE: NO
FAILURE_LAYER: draft-repair
REPLAY_FROM: draft-repair

---

SUMMARY:

The manuscript takes a credible angle â€” Woodside's multi-year industrial AI build as a case study in what load-bearing AI dependency actually means â€” and executes it with more editorial discipline than most SEO-term-driven drafts. It handles most of the factual record accurately, acknowledges the sponsored source in the body, and is transparent about its inference when connecting the three stories. It nonetheless fails publication on a set of bounded but specific defects: the summary borrows MIT Technology Review's editorial authority for what is disclosed in the body as a branded piece; the headline overpromises what a single sponsored interview can establish; one sentence makes an unsupported absolute claim about consumer AI; and the weather-sabotage section, while self-aware about being inference, uses that self-awareness as permission to make a framing move the sources don't actually support.

None of these defects require sourcing that isn't available or a thesis replacement. They require targeted repair to the summary field, headline, one sentence in the body, and the weather-sabotage paragraph.

---

FINDINGS:

The piece's core structure holds. It draws three distinct stories into a coherent argument about industrial AI dependency and re-pricing risk, cites each source for the specific claims it makes, and does not conflate the Woodside profile's claims with the Register's or the weather piece's. The editorial judgment to distinguish industrial AI from consumer productivity AI is analytically defensible and grounded in source material.

The most serious defect is in the summary/description field, which is the version of the piece that appears in feeds, social cards, and syndication packets. That field states: "MIT Technology Review positions the energy sector as a preview of AI functioning as a core operating layer." The body correctly discloses that editorial independence is not verifiable from the available source record â€” but the summary makes no such qualification. A reader encountering only the summary is given the impression that MIT Technology Review's editorial desk assessed the energy sector this way, when in fact the source is a transcript from the outlet's branded "Business Lab" podcast produced in partnership with Infosys. Attribution laundering occurs most consequentially where the disclosure is absent, and the summary is the highest-distribution surface.

The headline â€” "Shows What an Autonomous Enterprise Actually Requires" â€” makes a stronger claim than one VP's interview in a sponsored profile can establish. The source gives one practitioner's account of one company's journey. "What an autonomous enterprise actually requires" reads as a settled answer derived from independent evidence. It isn't.

In the body, the sentence "that consumer-facing AI never had to meet" is an overstatement. The source does not assert this; it describes Woodside's specific context. Consumer AI has faced constraints â€” different ones, not none.

The weather-sabotage paragraph is the piece's most structurally questionable move. The manuscript acknowledges that "the two stories do not draw between each other" and offers the connection as something "the cases together make visible." That transparency is better than most, but it doesn't resolve the problem: the framing imports a risk narrative from a story about weather station tampering into an analysis of Woodside's AI dependency, and presents the juxtaposition as analytically meaningful. The weather sabotage piece is about manipulation of external observational inputs to forecasting systems; Woodside's dependency concern is about AI as an internal operational layer. These are meaningfully different risk surfaces, and presenting them as illustrative of "different levels" of the same phenomenon invents a comparative architecture the sources do not provide.

---

CLAIM AUDIT:

The claim that Woodside began AI adoption with predictive analytics and machine learning before generative AI tools is accurately sourced to the MIT Technology Review profile. The Andrew Melouney quote about "really clear, quite high-value use cases" appears verbatim in the source. His title as vice president for digital is accurate. The characterization of Woodside's 2015 start date for traditional AI tools is supported.

The Forrester survey count of "more than 2,600 business and technology decision-makers" matches the source. The claim that Anthropic, OpenAI, and GitHub shifted services toward usage-based billing in the last six months is sourced. The E7 license description as "bundling M365 Copilot and Agent 365 into a higher-cost tier" is slightly imprecise â€” the Register says it "bolts M365 Copilot, Agent 365, and security tools onto E5" â€” but this is an acceptable simplification rather than a distortion.

The weather claims (utilities using forecasts to site solar/wind farms and price wholesale electricity; sabotage risk rising with prediction market incentives) are accurately represented from the MIT Technology Review weather piece.

The summary's claim that MIT Technology Review "positions the energy sector as a preview of AI functioning as a core operating layer" cannot be attributed to MIT Technology Review editorially; it should be attributed to the Insights-produced profile specifically.

No fabricated facts or invented causal claims were found in the body copy. The piece does not flatten distinct entities or regions. No figures are misquoted.

---

REQUIRED REPAIRS:

First: Revise the summary/description field to attribute the energy-sector framing to the specific profile and disclose the sponsored production context, consistent with the body's parenthetical. The current summary launders the MIT Technology Review masthead into a factual attribution that the body itself qualifies.

Second: Soften the headline. "Shows What an Autonomous Enterprise Actually Requires" should be brought in line with what the source supports â€” one company's VP account of one company's approach, produced in a sponsored context. The headline can retain analytical ambition without asserting settled conclusions.

Third: Remove or qualify "that consumer-facing AI never had to meet." The source does not assert this; the sentence overstates the contrast.

Fourth: Either cut the weather-sabotage paragraph or reframe it honestly as a speculative juxtaposition rather than an analytical distinction. If kept, it should be explicit that this connection is the writer's inference from two unrelated stories, not a relationship the sources establish. The current framing â€” "operates at a different level: AI not as one input among many, but as a continuous condition of operation itself â€” a distinction the two stories do not draw between each other, but that the cases together make visible" â€” is doing too much rhetorical work for sources that don't support the architecture it implies.

---

PIPELINE EVIDENCE:

The production run shows `writer.usage.calls: 0` and `writer.usage.tokens: 0`, which indicates the draft was not generated by the writer model in this run â€” it arrived pre-written or from a prior session. The packet correctly flagged the mixed-editorial-sponsored-source risk with a warning. The deterministic gate currently reads `publishable: true` and `findings: []`, indicating the automated standards review passed. The manuscript frontmatter carries `gates_clear: false`, which is a historical production state and not the current gate reading; per standing protocol, the current deterministic standards reading governs.

The packet's `mixed-editorial-sponsored-source-risk` warning was visible to the writer and is not a hidden constraint. The writer addressed it in the body but not in the summary. The failure is in execution, not in the information available.

---

PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:

The draft-repair defects here all stem from a single pattern: the writer applying a disclosure correctly in one location (parenthetical in the body) but not propagating it to higher-distribution surfaces (summary, headline). The pipeline should add a gate that checks whether the body carries a sponsored-source disclosure and, if so, flags whether the summary field independently carries equivalent qualification. A body-level disclosure does not protect readers who encounter only the summary card in a feed. That propagation check is currently absent and would have caught the primary defect here before editorial review.

The weather-sabotage paragraph illustrates a related pattern: the writer correctly narrates that a connection is inferential but then proceeds as if narrating the inference makes the analytical claim valid. Instructions might benefit from a more explicit constraint: a paragraph that identifies its reasoning as cross-source inference must either be cut or marked for editorial judgment before publication â€” it cannot self-approve by acknowledging its own speculative status.

---

END REPORT:

